From 83fe3c02bd8987e905c6a4cbf4d1b18662045e0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex17273747576777 <Alex17273747576777@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 11:46:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(i18n): stop hard-coding the feed cap in 15
 translations

too_many_in_feed quoted the limit as a literal number. base said 25 after the cap moved
to FeedLimits.MAX_SOURCES_PER_FEED, but the 15 translations that carry the string still
said 10 - the pre-2.8.3 legacy rule - so a Spanish, German, French, Japanese, Russian,
Portuguese, Arabic, Turkish, Indonesian, Tamil, Croatian, Chinese (both scripts),
Filipino or Assamese reader was told the app refuses at 10 sources when it actually
refuses at 25.

The number is now a %1$d token in all 16 locales and both rendering paths pass
FeedLimits.MAX_SOURCES_PER_FEED, so a locale cannot advertise a limit the code does not
enforce, and changing the constant updates every language at once.

Only the numeral was replaced in each translation; the wording is untouched, one line
per file.

Two things this repo already had made the change small: presentation-core exposes a
Composable stringResource(resource, vararg args) for the snackbar path in FeedTab, and
SourceFeedScreenModel already imported FeedLimits for hasTooManyFeeds().

The toast path needed a new overload in ToastExtensions. It takes formatArgs as a List
and NOT as vararg args: Any, because MigrationListScreen:43 and SettingsEhScreen:918
call context.toast(res, Toast.LENGTH_LONG) positionally and an Int would match both a
duration: Int parameter and a vararg Any, making those calls ambiguous. A List cannot
be mistaken for an Int, so every existing call site resolves exactly as it does today;
context.toast(res) and context.toast(text, duration) are untouched.

Verified by execution, not by reading: all 16 strings were extracted from the modified
XML (unescaping \' and XML entities) and formatted with java.util.Formatter under each
locale on JDK 17, which is the formatter moko's StringDesc.ResourceFormatted uses.
16 of 16 render the limit with no leftover token, and two of them now get locale-native
digits that a hand-written number could never have: Arabic renders 25 as U+0662 U+0665
and Assamese as U+09E8 U+09EB. XML well-formedness was re-checked after the edit and
each file differs from HEAD by exactly one line.

Known limitation, flagged rather than silently rewritten: Turkish attaches a
vowel-harmony suffix to the numeral (was 10'dan, now 25'dan). The suffix agrees with
the digit it follows, so no single form is correct for every value of the constant -
25 wants 'ten and 30 would want 'dan. A suffix-free phrasing such as 'En fazla %1$d
kaynak eklenebilir' would be robust, but rewriting a translation without a native
speaker is worse than disclosing it, so the numeral-only change stands and this is left
for whoever owns the Turkish strings.

Not verified: Gradle did not run here, so spotlessCheck and assembleDevDebug were not
executed and the Kotlin changes are not compiled. The .editorconfig constraints the
changed files are subject to were checked mechanically instead: UTF-8, 4-space indent,
max_line_length 120, no trailing whitespace, final newline present.
